Chunky sculpted timber make up the Oka chairs wide spacious frame. Designed to showcase the best of what nature has to offer its simple wool upholstery reveals the hemp rope suspension underneath. Soft forms and ample seating invites multiple ways to sit and relax while bold details and clever joinery carries a presence into any space it is placed. 

Part of [[SDCSC001: Why A Chair?]]

---

!! Where it comes from

Three materials. The intention was for each to come from somewhere you can drive to in an afternoon. Two do. The third doesn't right now. What they have is the best available, and they hope to bring it back closer.

* ''Wood.'' Fallen city trees, milled into lumber by [[Cambium Carbon]], out of Baltimore and the woods around it. The trees came down from disease, decay, disaster, or development.
* ''Wool.'' Undyed wool from [[Willow Creek Farm]] in Pennsylvania, where [[Melissa Smith]] grazes Shetland sheep and mills the wool on the farm. The cushion stuffing is [[Waste Wool]], the fibers too short or discolored to spin.
* ''Hemp.'' The cord under the seat, left showing. This is the weak link. See [[Hemp]]: a lot of effort locally to make hemp cord; then a rope maker found nearish-by in North Carolina closed, and for now the fiber comes from France. Hopefully a better solution is coming soon!

The chair is built at [[Open Works]], the Baltimore makerspace, by [[Common Object Studio]]. Every tool, material, and maker already existed when the first chair was drawn. The work was finding them.

!! Why it is low and wide

Two reasons, both from [[Justin Beitzel]].

The chunky parts come from the wood. City salvaged lumber has worm holes and knots, so the parts were made wide enough that nobody wonders whether a knot or a bit of character is structural. Most wood waste comes from cutting around those flaws, and you lose a big piece of the board around each mark.

The width of the arms was about use. The goal was a casual platform you could sling a leg over the side of, or sit cross-legged on to read.

//Source: correspondence with Justin Beitzel, October 2026.//

!! What it costs

$6,900, made to order, with a 5 year warranty. ([[okaterra.com|https://okaterra.com/products/the-oka-chair]], October 2026)

